PRETABLE -- #530 (lcaisalane) kill table 7, CLOSING PASS 5's REPAIR 6 (the forward-COUNT CHANNEL repair, 352 rows).
Written BEFORE the table: this file's mtime precedes the driver, every build log and every probe log.  MANDATORY
under the 2026-08-28 flagship ruling (any round shipping or EXTENDING a kill-table-backed detector), and recorded
as OUTPUT rather than asserted.

================================================================================================================
INSTRUMENT
================================================================================================================
  regress/lca_isalane_probe.py after repair 6 -- 352 rows, frozen by the driver as lca_isalane_probe.table7.py.
  Its own post-repair run is 352/352 (repair6_20260916/probe_repair6_fix.out, LCAISALANE_DONE, RUNNER_RC=0).
  The 352 decompose EXACTLY, and the decomposition is derived from the run's own row names, not transcribed:
      185  every row that existed before repair 6          (unchanged; `printed' left BYTE-identical)
      150  the four 8253 sessions  pit0 37 / pit1 37 / pit2 37 / pitT 39
       16  `kbcmd'  = 4 widths x {alive, pre, pop, post}
        1  `stub3 z1'
      ---
      352
  DERIVER for that census (re-run it rather than trusting the numbers):
      grep -E '^[a-z0-9].* (ok|FAIL)$' <log> | cut -c1-16 | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

================================================================================================================
WHAT CHANGED -- repair 6, applied in FOUR PARTS, per FABLE_C5_RULING_20260915.md
================================================================================================================
  1. HARNESS, backward-compatible by construction.  `nth(t, reg, n)' and `n_prints(t, reg)' added; `build()' accepts
     a LIST of registers per phase; `run()'s `want_regs' entry may be ("t3", 18) meaning at-least-18 prints.
     *** `printed()' IS BYTE-UNCHANGED *** -- that is what makes the 185 legacy rows a carried measurement rather
     than a re-measurement.
  2. FOUR 8253 SESSIONS (150 COUNT rows) -- the new CHANNEL.
  3. `kbcmd' (16 COUNT rows) -- the 8042 reply queue as a count oracle, at all four widths.
  4. `stub3 z1' (1 row) -- the lane-3 x ZERO-STORE joint.
  Plus the ROW TAXONOMY banners over `iir'/`cmos' and the contract re-derivation (records; no row).
  NO EXISTING SESSION'S COMMAND LIST CHANGED.

  WHY THE CHANNEL MOVED, and it is the whole warrant for repair 6 -- closing pass 5's central finding:
      A LATCH SEPARATES ZERO ACCESSES FROM AT LEAST ONE.  IT CAN NEVER SEPARATE ONE FROM TWO -- which is exactly
      and only what a forward-COUNT class asks.
      RULE: A FORWARD-COUNT ROW NEEDS A COUNTER OR AN ALTERNATOR, NEVER A LATCH.
  CMOS REG C (dev_mc146818.c:565) and ns16550 IIR (dev_ns16550.c:211-213) are one-shot LATCHES, so lanes 1 and 2
  had no count carrier and the emulator-TODO pckbc stub was sole carrier at twelve cells.  The 8253 flip-flops
  (dev_8253.c:280-285) are ALTERNATORS: `wr_msb[]'/`rd_msb[]' toggle on EVERY counter access while
  (mode_byte & 0x30) == I8253_TIMER_16BIT, and a control word REWINDS both (:320-321) so each phase self-starts.

================================================================================================================
TARGETING
================================================================================================================
  Repair 6 is ADDS-ONLY to the detector: no existing session's command list changed, so by the targeting rule no
  table 1-6 kill is moved and none is re-run for its own sake.  Table 7 nevertheless runs ALL EIGHTEEN LEGS on the
  merged 352-row instrument, because the question this pass owes is not "did the old rows move" but
  *** DOES THE EIGHT-CELL ENUMERATION CLOSE ***, and that is answerable only on the instrument that claims it.
  It also discharges, BY CONSTRUCTION, the three measurements closing pass 5 named as NOT REACHED:
      Wdrop and Rdrop were never run against the prototype 8253 rows;
      W23 was never run against the 8253 rows;
      `kbcmd' did not exist as rows at all.
  Every one of the three is a full column of this table.

