================================================================================================================
PRE-TABLE CHECK 5 -- THE PRESCRIBED-ROW DIFF (the 2026-08-28 amendment: a rewritten row list is DIFFED against
this block's own prescriptions, not merely extended)
================================================================================================================
  PRETABLE 6 prescribed no rows that repair 6 could have dropped: its prescriptions were all discharged by table 6
  and its POSTTABLE records them.  Repair 6 ADDS 167 rows and REMOVES none -- verified by the decomposition at the
  top of this file, where all 185 legacy rows are still present and `printed()' is byte-unchanged.  So the
  prescribed-row diff is EMPTY IN THE DIRECTION THAT MATTERS (nothing lost), and that is a checked statement rather
  than an assumption: 352 - 167 = 185, and the legacy families still appear in the 352-row census above.

================================================================================================================
PRE-TABLE CHECK 4 -- CHANNEL.  Per row family: the mutant it kills, and the observable that differs under it
================================================================================================================
  pit<N> alive          the session reached the guest at all (a flat sentinel read).  Kills no mutant; it is an
                        IDENTITY row and reds when the session did not run.
  pit<N> live1/live2    two consecutive flat reads of the counter port return DIFFERENT halves (LSB then MSB).
                        Kills `noff'.  Observable: the alternator advancing.  *** This is the row that makes every
                        other pit row mean something: without it a green count row could mean the oracle is dead. ***
  pit<N> ps5a w<k>/B    a NONZERO (0x5a) store of width k through the lca lane; the B row reads the WRITE
                        flip-flop's parity after it.  Kills the write arm's DROP (Wdrop), DUPLICATE (WWall), SWAP
                        (WXR), ADD-OTHER (Wpure/x_wfallthru).  Observable: which half the next flat read returns.
  pit<N> ps00 w<k>/B    the same channel with a ZERO stored byte.  Kills the DATA-GATED write forms (`dNZtail's
                        shape at the direct widths; any `data[ofs] ? ... :' fallthrough).  Observable: identical
                        channel, different data -- which is what turns the zero/nonzero axis from a MARGINAL over
                        all cells into a PER-CELL joint.
  pit<N> pl5a r<k>/B    a load of width k; the B row reads the READ flip-flop's parity.  Kills the read arm's DROP
                        (Rdrop), DUPLICATE (RD2post), SWAP (RXW), ADD-OTHER (RWall, RWtail).
  pit<N> pl00 r<k>/B    the same with the DELIVERED byte zero.  *** This is the row `RD2z' needs: RD2z gates the
                        duplicate on the delivered byte being 0 and escaped 185/185 precisely because no count row
                        was ever driven with a zero delivered byte. ***
  pit<N> pw r1/r2       a READ checked against the WRITE flip-flop (and the converse).  Kills the MIXED shapes --
                        an access that emits a forward in the OTHER direction -- which a same-direction parity row
                        is blind to BY CONSTRUCTION, because the two flip-flops are separate.
  pitT ps/pl * t7/t6/t5/t3 (+B)
                        the same channels at the SPLIT TAIL widths 7/6/5/3 at lane 0.  Kills WWtail, WW6, dNZtail
                        -- the three cells that escaped 185/185 because the tails carried only VALUE rows.
  pitT pw t6/t3 (+B)    cross-direction parity at the tails.
  kbcmd <w> alive       the 8042 answered at all.
  kbcmd <w> pre         DIB is SET before the pop: at least one reply was queued.  Kills the write arm's DROP and
                        SWAP (no command byte reaches the 8042 -> no reply).
  kbcmd <w> pop         the popped byte is the expected reply.
  kbcmd <w> post        DIB is CLEAR after exactly one pop: NOT MORE THAN ONE reply was queued.  Kills the write
                        arm's DUPLICATE at the direct widths.  *** `pre' and `post' are the two halves a latch can
                        never give you: `pre' separates zero from one, `post' separates one from two. ***
  stub3 z1              ONE ZERO store at lane 3 on the stub's access-parity alternator.  Kills `cdxZL3' (lane 3 x
                        zero data), which is the one cell the 8253 channel structurally cannot reach.

  *** EVERY ROW ABOVE NAMES A CHANNEL THAT CAN VARY UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT.  The one family that could not -- a
  count row resting on a LATCH -- is exactly what repair 6 removed from the count role, and the `cmos'/`iir' rows
  are RETAINED as VALUE/POSITION rows with the contract saying so. ***

