    RD2post      24     42    0                                                 0    286/352
    RXW          42     53    8  (`pre'+`pop': the write becomes a read, so no
                                  command byte is ever delivered)               0    249/352
    WXR          45     32    8  (same: the write is emitted as a read)         0    267/352
    Rdrop        58     UNMEASURED -- predicted 53 (the read-parity rows)       0    ~241/352  *LOW CONFIDENCE*
    Wdrop        49     UNMEASURED -- predicted 64 (the write-parity rows)      8    ~231/352  *LOW CONFIDENCE*
    Wpure        32     64    4                                                 1    251/352

  *** THE THREE `UNMEASURED' ROWS ARE THE POINT OF THIS TABLE AND THEIR PREDICTIONS ARE THE LEAST TRUSTWORTHY
  NUMBERS IN IT.  A miss there is a PREDICTION error and is reported as one; a miss on a CARRIED component means
  the merge changed an existing row's behaviour, which would be a far more serious finding about repair 6. ***

  THE CLOSING CONDITION, stated before the run so it cannot be fitted afterwards:
      This pass CLOSES only if EVERY mutant of the fix is KILLED and the zero is carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR --
      eighteen legs over the eight-cell product {write, read} x {DROP, SWAP, DUPLICATE, ADD-OTHER}.  A leg at
      352/352 that is a mutant of `dev_lca.c' is an ESCAPE and the pass does NOT close.  `cdxZL3' at 351/352 is
      the enumeration's own check on itself; `cdxZL3' at 352/352 means `stub3 z1' is vacuous and repair 6 is
      incomplete at the one cell it was added for.
      `noff' green would mean the 150 new rows are vacuous and NOTHING else in this table may be believed.

  A red row outside its prediction is RE-RUN ALONE before it is believed.
